// @emit-error the reassigned function binding `f` is called at more than one argument type
// THE GENERIC-FREE TWIN, and the cell that says what the reject is worth: on master this
// program COMPILED, LOADED, RAN, and printed `1 x 1.5 3` where the program says `102` —
// the silent wrong-answer class, not invalid wasm.
//
// `monoMakeInstance`'s APPEND rung (a second distinct pinned SHAPE) rewrote the callee
// `Ident` from the binding `f` to the appended instance of the binding's INITIALIZER,
// turning a `call_ref` through the cell into a by-name direct call — so the `f = …` write
// between them was invisible and both later calls ran the FIRST lambda. Its sibling
// registry-hit rung has carried the `monoNameIsAssigned` arm since #1821; this rung never
// did. No generic is involved: an un-annotated lambda parameter is an inference hole, which
// is all `monoGen == 1` needs.
//
// The counter is the whole point of the fixture. Without it the two identity lambdas make
// the WRONG answer indistinguishable from the right one, which is why this survived #1821's
// sweep — its 38 cells all read a value the two arms agree on.
let n = 0
let f = (a) => {
  n = n + 1
  return a
}
print(f(1))
print(f("x"))
f = (a) => {
  n = n + 100
  return a
}
print(f(1.5))
print(n)
